WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.080
The reference catalog maps recurring competitive programming patterns to their canonical algorithms and templates.

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:14.658
Start by identifying whether the problem asks for an extremum, a count, a reachability check, or an explicit construction.

00:00:14.658 --> 00:00:19.440
Cross-check input limits immediately to narrow the candidate data structures.

00:00:19.708 --> 00:00:27.481
Harder division problems rarely test a single algorithm in isolation; instead they chain two standard primitives together.

00:00:27.481 --> 00:00:33.407
Flattening a tree with an Euler tour turns a subtree problem into a Fenwick tree range query.

00:00:33.407 --> 00:00:39.268
Binary searching the answer turns an optimization problem into a simple greedy or BFS check.

00:00:39.542 --> 00:00:47.343
When a submission fails on a hidden test, write a tiny random input generator and a ten-line brute-force solver.

00:00:47.343 --> 00:00:52.428
Run both on thousands of ten-element inputs until their outputs disagree.

00:00:52.428 --> 00:00:57.862
Tracing a five-element counterexample exposes the broken edge case in minutes.

00:00:58.125 --> 00:01:06.516
Consistent rating growth comes from solving problems slightly above your comfort zone and upsolving every contest within twenty-four hours.

00:01:06.516 --> 00:01:11.526
When you read an editorial, close it before writing the code yourself from scratch.

00:01:11.526 --> 00:01:16.416
Log the key observation that unlocked each problem so you recognize it next time.
